}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339862 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339863 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339864 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339865 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339866 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1041 "selection" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339863 13194139535248 true] [17592186339861 1110 #uuid "c914888b-9af6-554b-ae9b-58d34794b8a1" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339862 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535248 true] [17592186339862 1111 #uuid "7a5f0726-4897-5532-acbc-7f2bd9c03f2a" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339863 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339863 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535248 true] [17592186339863 1111 #uuid "deb637da-a986-53da-8795-0fafd7c206f2" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339864 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535248 true] [17592186339864 1111 #uuid "1cb12ce2-b7c0-5b90-9e9e-5f012568424b" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339865 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535248 true] [17592186339865 1111 #uuid "fd222e95-2a78-5911-86b7-a9db8fdbe8ba" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339866 1034 "II only" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535248 true] [17592186339866 1111 #uuid "a31525d9-fd0a-5f3d-884f-d6254cfa35c2" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339868 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1089 "q-125668" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/6ed50407066da9fc213202ccff8c8be06c89d2dce0f5dfe07877cab77c98b3ec.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1113 #uuid "7226f430-9063-56c7-a03b-1f2aa7e4fc63" 13194139535248 true] [17592186339867 1145 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/045f6867a50dff0c162376015cc4651de796d0a637643b4b5ba402ef55dde4fa.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation 